<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francis I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1494">1494</a>–<a href="/years/1547">1547</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francis I was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from 1515 to 1547, a patron of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> and a rival of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>. The voyages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacques-cartier">Jacques Cartier</a> to the Gulf of St Lawrence in 1534 were undertaken in his name, beginning French claims in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> that underlay the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Italian Wars from 1494 French kings fought Spain and the Habsburgs, winning at the Battle of Marignano in 1515 but losing <b>Francis I</b> as a prisoner at the Battle of Pavia in 1525.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…at Basel in 1516; a College of the Three Languages for Latin, Greek and Hebrew was founded at Leuven in 1517; and in 1530 <b>Francis I</b> of France, urged by Guillaume Budé, appointed royal lecturers in Greek and Hebrew in Paris, the beginning of the Collège de France.</blockquote><a href="/movement/renaissance-humanism">Renaissance humanism · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Charles V's troops routed the French outside Pavia, and King <b>Francis I</b> was taken prisoner.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-24">24 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>It temporarily ended the struggle between <b>Francis I</b> and Emperor Charles V for control of northern Italy.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-21">21 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The electors chose Charles I of Spain over <b>Francis I</b> of France.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-28">28 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Leonardo died at Clos Lucé, the manor near Amboise given to him by <b>Francis I</b>. Painter of the Mona Lisa and The Last Supper, he also left thousands of pages of notes on anatomy, engineering and nature.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-2">2 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
